WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:05.400
Who finances the logging trucks that haul and the tankers that carefully carry?

00:06.000 --> 00:08.660
If it moves our country forward, we're behind it.

00:09.140 --> 00:11.700
UDC, New Zealand's finance company.

00:12.100 --> 00:14.840
We've got money to lend. Lots of money.

00:16.060 --> 00:19.240
IHF Health Club. What your day should be.

00:19.720 --> 00:22.980
The days are getting lighter and suddenly you feel like moving again.

00:23.460 --> 00:29.320
At IHF, premium training equipment, yoga, pilates, ride and high energy group fitness

00:29.320 --> 00:33.440
give you everything you need to put a little spring back in your day.

00:33.920 --> 00:38.240
And when you're done, recover in the sauna and cold plunge or stay for a coffee.

00:39.120 --> 00:42.920
IHF Health Club. Your oasis in the heart of the crossing.

00:43.300 --> 00:44.560
It's your everyday luxury.

00:53.520 --> 00:58.560
Welcome to the Economy of Everything brought to you by CMC Markets.

00:58.560 --> 01:05.960
Tamsin, we've just had some new stats out today on how tough things are for the hospitality businesses out there.

01:06.280 --> 01:07.640
When's the last time you went out to dinner?

01:08.180 --> 01:11.460
Well, it was actually just a week ago for a family birthday.

01:11.740 --> 01:14.520
But I have to say it was pretty grim.

01:14.800 --> 01:18.360
I was at a restaurant in Browns Bay and it was only half full.

01:18.720 --> 01:21.960
And literally they squashed us all into the back half of the restaurant

01:21.960 --> 01:26.020
to make it look like it was probably, you know, doing better than what it was.

01:26.160 --> 01:28.320
But pretty quiet for a Saturday night.

01:28.480 --> 01:31.280
There's no question that, you know, like people will have picked up the vibe.

01:31.480 --> 01:32.720
It's been tough in Auckland.

01:33.000 --> 01:33.580
It's winter.

01:34.340 --> 01:38.060
We've heard lots of stories of hospitality businesses going under.

01:38.120 --> 01:39.940
I mean, what are the stats telling us?

01:39.940 --> 01:49.120
So we've got some data from Centrix that's just come out showing that actually nearly 3,000 hospitality businesses ceased trading in the last year.

01:49.280 --> 01:51.100
And that's quite a lot.

01:51.120 --> 01:52.620
So I'd even imagine 3,000.

01:53.000 --> 01:54.520
I guess we're not just talking about restaurants.

01:54.700 --> 01:56.360
It must be every bar, pub.

01:56.360 --> 01:57.080
This is everything.

01:57.320 --> 02:03.660
This is restaurants, takeaways, you know, bars, anything in that sector.

02:03.660 --> 02:07.500
But that was a 38% increase on the year before.

02:07.780 --> 02:09.200
In terms of how many are going under?

02:09.280 --> 02:09.440
Yeah.

02:09.580 --> 02:10.740
Well, these aren't going under.

02:10.820 --> 02:12.200
These have just ceased trading.

02:12.260 --> 02:12.460
Sure.

02:12.820 --> 02:21.600
So the ones that have gone under, that's about 420 around about companies in the hospitality sector that have gone into liquidation.

02:21.640 --> 02:24.780
That was up from 297 a year earlier.

02:24.780 --> 02:33.240
But basically, if you're a hospo business, you're about three times more likely to be going into liquidation than other average businesses.

02:33.660 --> 02:33.720
Wow.

02:33.820 --> 02:47.140
And, I mean, it's a fine line between going under and just going out of business because there must be owners out there who are just hanging on, hanging on, not making enough money going, no, we're just going to walk away from this lease or something like that.

02:47.180 --> 02:49.640
I mean, that's where some of those stats will be coming from.

02:49.760 --> 02:52.600
I mean, it doesn't paint a great picture of the economy, does it?

02:52.600 --> 02:53.560
I mean, we'll get into this later.

02:53.680 --> 02:57.960
But, you know, we started to hear the economists sounding a bit upbeat again.

02:58.800 --> 02:59.460
Yeah, we have.

02:59.460 --> 03:07.440
I guess we had, you know, we've had a little bit of data come out of ANZ, haven't we, with business confidence showing that things are a little bit upbeat.

03:07.580 --> 03:14.080
They're sort of indicating that, you know, the expectation is things are as bad as they've got and they're getting better.

03:14.080 --> 03:17.680
But I don't know if a lot of people would be feeling that yet.

03:17.820 --> 03:18.060
Yeah.

03:18.160 --> 03:29.440
I mean, I noticed Westpac and their chief economist, Kelly Echold, he's kind of wrapped up everything that's been happening, the high frequency data, and concluded that the recovery is underway.

03:29.440 --> 03:36.820
I've been a bit hesitant to come out with a big story saying the recovery's back, green shoots or whatever, because we've just been burned so many times.

03:37.020 --> 03:44.060
And, you know, I know we're in central Auckland here and perhaps it's a bit more lively in some other parts of the country.

03:44.260 --> 03:54.940
But, you know, gee, I hope spring helps pick things up because if you're out and about in town and at the moment, it doesn't feel like an economic recovery is underway yet.

03:54.940 --> 03:56.780
No, it doesn't, definitely not.

03:57.260 --> 04:02.140
And, you know, hospos down, but, you know, obviously retail's doing it tough as well.

04:02.880 --> 04:06.500
I don't know about you, but my local mall has lots of empty shops in it.

04:06.800 --> 04:10.900
They sort of, you know, try and cover it up, make it look like there's a front there.

04:11.180 --> 04:16.080
But, yeah, so hospos, retail, construction, you know, they're all feeling it.

04:16.740 --> 04:24.760
That centric starter did also show that construction was the – the construction sector had the highest number of liquidations over the last year.

04:24.760 --> 04:27.620
But that isn't growing as fast.

04:27.760 --> 04:29.860
So that sort of seems to have stabilised.

04:30.020 --> 04:33.080
So maybe there is, you know, some good news.

04:33.460 --> 04:35.420
Yeah, well, it will be jobs, won't it?

04:35.520 --> 04:42.780
And the trouble is, you know, it creates a mixed picture for people trying to get a sense from the media of what's happening with the economy.

04:42.780 --> 04:47.740
Because the economists do look at front-end data and they say, well, that's a bit positive.

04:47.880 --> 04:50.040
The confidence looking forward is positive.

04:51.580 --> 05:01.360
You know, but then there's this other stuff that the liquidation numbers, which we're talking about here, you know, and overall the unemployment rate.

05:01.520 --> 05:03.740
These are the numbers that take longest to turn.

05:03.740 --> 05:10.480
So you'll get economists talking about things starting to improve and say, well, there's economic growth.

05:10.540 --> 05:12.280
We might even see, you know, GDP growth.

05:13.560 --> 05:23.720
But out there at the coalface, people are still feeling, you know, the blunt force of, you know, the labour market downturn and all that sort of stuff.

05:23.800 --> 05:25.380
So that'll take a while to turn.

05:25.600 --> 05:27.820
Yeah, I think there's going to be a long tail of liquidations.

05:27.960 --> 05:32.320
I don't think that there's any sign of, you know, that's dropping anytime soon.

05:32.320 --> 05:38.400
I think there is a lot of companies that are just kind of struggling, hoping things are going to get better, really hoping for a pickup.

05:38.780 --> 05:54.820
But, you know, if that doesn't happen or, you know, they just can't stay on top of their debt levels, then, you know, sometimes the only option, you know, is to go into liquidation or wind up just because they can't keep paying their bills.

05:55.020 --> 05:59.760
And after a while, you know, the people like the Inland Revenue come calling.

06:00.340 --> 06:00.700
Absolutely.

06:00.700 --> 06:16.080
I mean, I think it is important and OK to lean into some of that optimism and good news at the front end of the economic data because that helps boost the confidence that will eventually help the, you know, the rest of it turn.

06:16.300 --> 06:19.500
Because, you know, if we stay downbeat, then it becomes self-fulfilling.

06:19.500 --> 06:21.540
So I guess it's a difficult balance.

06:21.880 --> 06:29.640
But, you know, perhaps, you know, we're going to hear sort of two different stories, particularly leading up to the election.

06:29.860 --> 06:39.880
And it will become quite political which story people decide to put the foot down on, whether it's the sort of the recoveries underway, it's all going well, which I'm sure we're going to hear from the government.

06:39.880 --> 06:46.740
Or the liquidations, the unemployment, no, we're in terrible shape, which you'll probably hear from the opposition.

06:47.520 --> 06:48.340
Yeah, that's right.

06:48.420 --> 06:54.260
And, of course, the truth will lie somewhere, you know, and amongst all of that, it's a little bit of both, I'm sure.

06:54.260 --> 06:57.380
And we are getting really close to that election.

06:57.640 --> 06:59.860
Well, I guess we're still, you know, quite a few weeks out.

06:59.940 --> 07:04.360
But in terms of, you know, the parliament breaking up, that's fast approaching.

07:04.480 --> 07:07.280
There's not many sitting days, you know, left down there now.

07:07.280 --> 07:11.660
So I think we're going to see, you know, the political policies and rhetoric stepping up.

07:11.980 --> 07:20.420
Yeah, I mean, I think it's going to come down to rhetoric because I don't think, you know, even with some of the early signs of recovery and things,

07:20.420 --> 07:28.940
I don't think the average person, average Joe, whoever he is, is going to feel it, particularly because it's, you know, in the end,

07:29.160 --> 07:35.720
that sort of sense that the economy is better than it was usually comes down to sort of your wages getting ahead of inflation.

07:35.720 --> 07:43.640
And I think that equation has still got a way to go before it sort of turns to sort of wages being ahead of the inflation rate.

07:43.840 --> 07:44.440
Yeah, definitely.

07:44.720 --> 07:46.940
You know, you're not feeling richer.

07:47.360 --> 07:49.780
You're not feeling richer from your house price going up either.

07:49.780 --> 07:54.400
So, you know, there's not many things to make you feel like, oh, everything's getting better.

07:54.720 --> 07:55.400
Well, yeah.

07:55.720 --> 07:58.040
Well, I mean, interesting you mentioned the house prices.

07:58.200 --> 08:01.780
I think we will touch on this next.

08:01.940 --> 08:09.680
But, you know, people, there's been an upside to the downturn, if you like, in terms of what's happened to New Zealand's debt picture.

08:09.840 --> 08:12.740
We've put out our Nation of Debt series this week.

08:12.840 --> 08:16.060
And, yeah, when we come back, let's dig into that.

08:16.420 --> 08:16.700
Definitely.

08:19.780 --> 08:24.860
Who finances the logging trucks that haul and the tankers that carefully carry?

08:25.400 --> 08:28.120
If it moves our country forward, we're behind it.

08:28.740 --> 08:31.180
UDC, New Zealand's finance company.

08:31.620 --> 08:33.100
We've got money to lend.

08:33.540 --> 08:34.320
Lots of money.

08:34.320 --> 08:38.640
The following advertisement is placed by New Zealand Council of Trade Unions.

08:38.880 --> 08:41.040
Christopher Luxon had one job.

08:41.140 --> 08:42.660
Help Kiwis with the cost of living.

08:42.820 --> 08:43.660
He failed.

08:43.880 --> 08:46.440
For three years, the middle in New Zealand has gone backward.

08:46.660 --> 08:47.480
He's out of touch.

08:47.580 --> 08:50.680
He says $60 is the cost of a weekly grocery shop.

08:50.800 --> 08:53.480
His choices are making it harder for us to make ends meet.

08:53.560 --> 08:55.260
It's time to vote for change.

08:55.400 --> 08:57.540
Don't risk six years of Luxon.

08:57.540 --> 09:11.660
Welcome back to the Economy of Everything podcast brought to you by CMC Markets.

09:11.780 --> 09:15.900
So, Liam, Nation of Debt, we've been doing it now, this series, for 10 years.

09:16.360 --> 09:17.620
What have we found this time around?

09:17.620 --> 09:23.880
Yeah, so this is the series where we tally up all of the gross national debt.

09:24.000 --> 09:25.320
So, this is important to emphasise.

09:25.500 --> 09:31.920
So, it's all of our mortgage debt, all of our personal finance debt, credit cards,

09:33.040 --> 09:39.300
local government debt and central government debt and business debt and agricultural debt.

09:39.360 --> 09:43.680
And we throw that all in to get a gross figure, which always makes a big, scary number.

09:44.680 --> 09:46.620
And it's no different.

09:46.620 --> 09:47.180
No different.

09:47.320 --> 09:48.460
We're approaching a trillion.

09:48.880 --> 09:57.360
So, I was thinking it was $937 billion, something like that, is the gross figure for our national debt.

09:57.460 --> 10:09.220
But interestingly, what it shows is that in a particular, in a couple of key areas where the Reserve Bank had been very nervous about precarious debt levels,

10:09.220 --> 10:14.180
that was mortgage debt and agricultural debt, things have started to balance.

10:14.180 --> 10:24.580
The rate of growth in those debt areas, debt sectors has really flattened out because, well, I guess in the ag sector, it's all good news.

10:24.700 --> 10:26.940
They've got commodity prices are high.

10:26.940 --> 10:29.060
Well, they had this big injection of money, didn't they?

10:29.060 --> 10:29.200
Yeah, yeah.

10:29.200 --> 10:31.140
And that's helped them, you know, bring that debt down.

10:31.160 --> 10:32.580
They've paid down debt and that's good.

10:33.280 --> 10:35.000
Housing market, not so amazing.

10:35.140 --> 10:37.820
It's more a case that the house prices have fallen.

10:38.220 --> 10:39.500
The market's really sluggish.

10:39.600 --> 10:41.340
People just aren't borrowing as much.

10:41.340 --> 10:43.000
The banks aren't lending as much.

10:43.100 --> 10:50.820
So, the rate of mortgage debt growth has slowed and we've seen stabilisation there.

10:50.980 --> 10:53.360
But that's still probably pretty good if you think about it.

10:53.440 --> 11:03.020
What the Reserve Bank used to worry about was sort of a sudden collapse where people went into negative equity and couldn't repay the debt.

11:03.020 --> 11:17.720
So, I think while there's been examples in the media of people who bought houses in that period 2021 when things were booming who are really underwater, beyond that there was a sort of a bubble in 2021.

11:18.220 --> 11:25.600
And the prices have eased back and are now just sort of tracking sideways down a bit when you throw inflation in.

11:25.600 --> 11:28.800
But a lot of people already had equity there.

11:28.860 --> 11:35.160
That doesn't seem to be the level of sort of distressed mortgages and mortgagee sales.

11:35.260 --> 11:35.540
I don't know.

11:35.640 --> 11:36.980
Have you seen numbers around that?

11:37.160 --> 11:37.720
Yeah, I have.

11:37.720 --> 11:45.380
So, you know, it looks like we're on track for around about, you know, 400 mortgagee sales so far this year.

11:46.940 --> 11:48.100
And that's pretty low.

11:48.280 --> 11:51.560
After the GFC, we got up to something like $3,000 in one year.

11:51.640 --> 11:55.080
And that was the sort of peak mortgagee sales period.

11:55.600 --> 12:01.700
And I guess what happened after that is that the government, the Reserve Bank, you know, a lot of changes were brought in.

12:02.020 --> 12:09.680
Things like LVR's loan-to-value ratios, which basically limit how much the banks can lend to people with low deposits.

12:10.440 --> 12:13.840
And more recently, in the last few years, debt-to-income caps.

12:13.840 --> 12:19.800
So, that's, you know, you can only borrow, say, up to six times your income or five times your income.

12:19.800 --> 12:19.980
Yep.

12:20.620 --> 12:34.680
Which I think is great because that does mean when we, you know, have a slowdown or, you know, things go backwards that, you know, fewer people have borrowed, I guess, to the extremes and to the point where, you know, they can't cope.

12:34.680 --> 12:43.960
Well, they have to say, and this is something the banks have always told me as well, that the key sort of determining factor on whether, you know, you can pay that mortgage is your job.

12:44.300 --> 12:44.540
Yeah.

12:44.740 --> 12:44.920
Yeah.

12:45.180 --> 12:46.780
Unemployment is a big, big one.

12:46.900 --> 12:53.820
I mean, but, you know, 400 mortgagee sales, you say, and it was up to 3,000 after the GFC.

12:53.820 --> 13:04.320
Well, you know, I think broadly when we step back, we're talking about an economy that is, you know, struggling as much as we were after the GFC in a lot of ways.

13:04.420 --> 13:14.000
I mean, people are starting to talk back to sort of some of the problems we went through in the late 70s and the early 90s when it comes to, you know, an extended period of downturn.

13:14.140 --> 13:16.560
The GFC was more of a short, sharp shock though, wasn't it?

13:17.240 --> 13:20.160
It hit hard and then there was a bounce.

13:20.480 --> 13:21.360
Yeah, it did hit hard.

13:21.360 --> 13:38.040
I mean, I remember back to that time and, you know, I do remember a lot of people losing their jobs or like also, I guess, you know, sort of having their hours scaled back, all those kinds of things that flow through and, you know, that would have made it that much harder to pay a mortgage.

13:38.220 --> 13:40.220
So I'm sure that was part of it.

13:40.500 --> 13:42.580
But, you know, I mean, it was tricky as well.

13:42.580 --> 13:51.800
I guess a lot of that time, you know, in New Zealand we didn't have the, you know, the banks collapsing but we did have, you know, the finance sector collapsing.

13:52.360 --> 14:00.680
So, you know, that flow through to people with, you know, with deposits that were secured over, you know, with finance companies.

14:00.680 --> 14:08.440
So, I guess as we talked about earlier, you know, some of the issues in the economy now are more that sort of retail, hospitality, small business end.

14:09.500 --> 14:17.700
You know, on the housing side, you know, okay, things have come off really sharply since the sort of steep peak end of 21.

14:18.380 --> 14:21.940
But, you know, just look at the way the population is ageing.

14:22.000 --> 14:29.380
There is a large pool of people with a lot of positive equity or even have paid off their mortgages, just older people.

14:29.380 --> 14:44.600
So it's important to remember when we look at that gross national debt figure, as horrible as nearly a trillion dollars looks, is there's actually something like, you know, $2 trillion of net household wealth in New Zealand.

14:44.860 --> 14:48.500
So, you know, and most of that is housing stock.

14:48.620 --> 14:53.460
Our KiwiSaver will count and some wealthy people with some other managed funds will count.

14:53.460 --> 15:09.580
But that housing stock, we do as a nation have positive equity in our housing stock at least, which means that when the ratings agencies look at us, you know, we are still roughly on the right way around in terms of the debt equation.

15:09.780 --> 15:10.320
Yeah, that's right.

15:10.400 --> 15:17.460
I think, you know, Standard & Poor's recently did an assessment that basically looked at us and, you know, said that things were looking okay, looking on track.

15:17.460 --> 15:35.020
Not too many worries, no big changes, because obviously if, you know, they downgrade our credit rating, that, you know, means it makes it more expensive for our government to borrow, which is obviously not helpful given, you know, we do have quite a bit of government debt at the moment.

15:35.020 --> 15:38.840
Yeah, I mean, we're working through in the debt series, the different sectors.

15:39.020 --> 15:52.240
We've already run business debt, which is a bit of an odd one because that's sort of an area where you want to see a bit more debt because you want to see businesses borrowing to invest and expand.

15:52.800 --> 15:58.280
And, you know, healthy businesses are often quite upbeat about taking on debt.

15:58.340 --> 16:00.040
And that really hasn't been the case, right?

16:00.080 --> 16:02.520
It's been pretty flat, a little bit of a comeback.

16:02.740 --> 16:04.820
You know, we're up, there was about 5% growth.

16:04.820 --> 16:07.300
Yeah, that's right, about 5% growth in business debt.

16:07.580 --> 16:11.240
You know, and some suggesting that that is a little bit of an indicator of confidence.

16:11.420 --> 16:21.360
But I think some people also believe that a bit of that debt might have been linked to the investment boost policy that the government announced where, you know, you could buy something new and then write off.

16:21.540 --> 16:22.940
Right, so the new ute or whatever.

16:22.940 --> 16:24.620
Yeah, yeah, yeah, exactly.

16:24.800 --> 16:28.900
So maybe some of that investment brought forward a bit because of, you know, government policy.

16:29.220 --> 16:31.520
So maybe it's not going to be longer term.

16:32.200 --> 16:35.740
But, yes, I guess there is also a bit of division.

16:35.940 --> 16:43.140
Maybe bigger businesses are able to borrow, but smaller businesses maybe are borrowing to keep themselves going, keep themselves afloat.

16:43.180 --> 16:43.740
Stay alive.

16:43.900 --> 16:44.400
Stay alive.

16:44.480 --> 16:45.020
That's not great.

16:45.140 --> 16:46.700
That's the wrong sort of borrowing.

16:46.840 --> 16:48.180
That's not really sustainable, is it?

16:48.180 --> 16:54.820
No, well, because the thing is once you run out of headroom and you don't have any way to pay that debt back, then you get into real trouble.

16:55.400 --> 16:55.940
Yeah, sure.

16:56.080 --> 17:01.380
And just to come back to housing, because we've got a more in-depth look at that coming out or is out today.

17:01.380 --> 17:08.640
You know, I think the RBNZ is more or less feeling pretty good with the way that it's come off.

17:08.740 --> 17:14.360
I mean, I know if you bought a house in late 21, you wouldn't be happy with where you're at.

17:14.360 --> 17:31.820
But I think, you know, given that the Reserve Bank has those tools around restricting lending in its pocket if things start to come back and that the rate of mortgage growth is subdued, they must be feeling OK.

17:32.500 --> 17:32.960
Yeah, they are.

17:32.960 --> 17:38.000
I think the estimates are around about 2% of all mortgage debt is in that negative equity.

17:38.220 --> 17:42.000
So, you know, where people potentially have more debt than the value of their house.

17:42.080 --> 17:46.380
So it's pretty small and, you know, that would, you know, keep it contained.

17:46.920 --> 17:50.820
I guess that's the plan is not to, you know, let it get out of control.

17:50.820 --> 17:57.800
I know there is starting to be a little bit more of a rise in people, say, buying a house with a smaller deposit.

17:58.020 --> 18:01.600
So not having a 20% deposit, having a 10% or a 5% deposit.

18:01.600 --> 18:09.320
So I'm sure the Reserve Bank will be keeping, you know, a very close watch on that to make sure it doesn't, you know, get above the limits that it's set.

18:09.440 --> 18:11.800
And they can move those restrictions around, right?

18:11.880 --> 18:19.500
So at the moment, they're not really probably bumping up against the restrictions too much because there just isn't the huge demand for borrowing out there.

18:19.640 --> 18:20.280
Yeah, that's right.

18:20.340 --> 18:22.500
Those restrictions, they move around all the time.

18:22.740 --> 18:25.880
I think they do an assessment on them every six months or so.

18:26.400 --> 18:30.880
But obviously, they're a lever that they can pull if they, you know, they see it getting out of control.

18:30.880 --> 18:31.520
Yeah.

18:31.820 --> 18:40.800
And other parts of the debt equation, the obvious one, and we'll be running this shortly, is government debt, crown debt.

18:41.000 --> 18:42.620
I mean, everyone's got a view on that.

18:42.780 --> 18:44.980
It's highly political.

18:45.580 --> 18:51.320
I, you know, I guess we're going to hear a lot more about that in the run up to the election, aren't we?

18:51.520 --> 18:52.500
Yeah, we definitely are.

18:52.500 --> 19:00.300
In finance, many so-called Kiwi institutions are answerable to an Australian parent.

19:00.640 --> 19:06.360
So a lot of decisions are made by a bloke who has to shoe kangaroos off the golf course and not step on a snake while he's doing it.

19:06.540 --> 19:13.920
But with Pacific Invoice Finance, those decisions are being made by someone who broke his leg at Mount Hutt when he was 12 or learned to surf at Mount Maunganui.

19:13.920 --> 19:16.980
They understand the market you're in because they're in it too.

19:17.340 --> 19:21.680
New Zealand's largest, only nationwide, non-bank invoice finance company.

19:22.080 --> 19:23.300
Pacific Invoice Finance.

19:24.180 --> 19:25.320
Oceans of possibility.

19:25.320 --> 19:27.500
You know what's great about podcasts?

19:27.820 --> 19:28.760
There's no pressure.

19:29.060 --> 19:29.920
You can pause them.

19:30.220 --> 19:30.920
Take your time.

19:31.400 --> 19:37.300
And Easy Kitchens is giving you a little more time to pay for your dream kitchen with interest-free terms available.

19:37.780 --> 19:40.560
That's because Easy Kitchens know the importance of time.

19:40.860 --> 19:44.720
That's why they take the time to listen to what you want, work through your ideas,

19:44.980 --> 19:49.700
so when it's time to install, every cabinet and every benchtop fits first time.

19:49.700 --> 19:54.320
So if you want a new kitchen, now's the time to talk to Easy Kitchens and Appliances.

19:54.320 --> 19:57.500
T.C.'s fees and normal lending criteria apply.

19:57.940 --> 19:58.640
Easy Kitchens.

19:58.980 --> 20:01.080
Made here by us for you.

20:02.940 --> 20:07.040
Welcome back to the Economy of Everything, thanks to CMC Markets.

20:07.180 --> 20:14.880
So Liam, you know, a topic that's going to become pretty, you know, talked about in the next few weeks likely is government debt.

20:15.200 --> 20:24.180
I know you've already written a column about it, but obviously it's, you know, New Zealand's government debt has really crept up in recent years.

20:24.180 --> 20:32.780
Yeah, and I think it is sort of the thing that stands out as not greatly improving in the latest debt series.

20:32.860 --> 20:38.740
To quote Jhene Tribshane, our Wellington business editor who's done the deep dive for us,

20:38.740 --> 20:42.660
the books are still in a bad way six years on from the pandemic.

20:42.660 --> 20:52.640
And I think those who worry about government debt would argue that neither of the major parties are really going that hard at paying it down.

20:54.000 --> 21:00.020
Net core crown debt is something like 41.3% of GDP.

21:00.020 --> 21:10.200
And I think there's sort of different timeframes from Labour and national as to how and when they get it down to about 40% of GDP.

21:10.500 --> 21:17.020
Well, when you think back to the Michael Cullen and Bill English years, we were aiming for 20% of GDP.

21:18.080 --> 21:19.060
So that was a lot lower.

21:19.180 --> 21:21.780
And they used to, it was a lot more of a conservative approach.

21:21.780 --> 21:31.300
There was a sense that you needed money in the tank for GFC earthquakes or a pandemic, which as it turned out, we did.

21:31.460 --> 21:31.540
Yeah.

21:31.720 --> 21:39.320
And now some people may, you know, be concerned that we need to move a little bit faster in case there is another major shock.

21:39.380 --> 21:46.360
So we actually got down as low as 18.6% of GDP in 2019 before the pandemic hit.

21:46.360 --> 21:49.720
So, you know, it's up a lot since then.

21:49.840 --> 21:49.980
Yeah.

21:50.080 --> 21:53.900
I mean, I'll play devil's advocate here and say maybe too low.

21:54.040 --> 22:06.920
Like you could, you certainly, if you looked at, say, the state of hospitals, schools and infrastructure and things, you'd say, well, look, maybe the foot should have been down a bit more on spending on some of those areas earlier.

22:07.540 --> 22:09.820
But it probably has paid off.

22:09.820 --> 22:27.400
So one of the issues that I've been very hot on is that, you know, what we're going to see in the next few weeks is that both, all the major parties put out these fiscal projections and they're going to pick each other to bits and they're going to say, no, you've got a fiscal hole, you've got a fiscal hole.

22:27.540 --> 22:28.580
There's going to be holes everywhere.

22:28.720 --> 22:29.040
Yeah, yeah.

22:29.120 --> 22:34.780
And a lot of it's to do with, is just to do with the timeframes and the way that they define the debt.

22:34.840 --> 22:36.520
And there are a lot of different ways you can cut it.

22:36.520 --> 22:43.400
But, and so that I think is just an accounting debate and it drives me crazy.

22:43.580 --> 22:53.820
What I think is, where I think the issue is, because you've still got people saying, look, we should be borrowing more because we can borrow cheaply, investing in infrastructure and building the things that we need.

22:53.820 --> 23:05.120
Because obviously if you borrow money at a low rate, invest in something productive that earns you, you know, more at a higher rate than what you're borrowing, that's a productive investment.

23:05.120 --> 23:08.680
Yeah, and it creates jobs, you know, we build a new bridge, whatever.

23:08.980 --> 23:10.020
There's got to be people to do it.

23:10.060 --> 23:11.300
It's easy to say that.

23:11.560 --> 23:18.160
I just think there is a lack of trust in the government of any stripes to achieve that.

23:18.220 --> 23:31.640
So people are nervous about taking on more debt and seeing, you know, this idea that it'll kind of go into projects and things that just don't seem to result in any payoff down the track.

23:31.640 --> 23:47.560
Now, I'm not sure if that's entirely fair, but after the past decade or so of back and forth on infrastructure, you know, there was a lot of necessary COVID stimulus, but then there's a lot of debate about how long that stimulus went on and whether the money was wasted there.

23:47.560 --> 23:54.200
I think the public is sceptical about governments spending efficiently.

23:54.380 --> 23:58.980
And I think that's, you know, what's important with debt is how you spend the money you borrow.

23:59.100 --> 24:08.340
If you take out a student loan and invest it in an engineering degree or a medical degree and you become a doctor, it's going to pay off or an engineering degree.

24:08.340 --> 24:20.160
You know, but if you take out a student loan, do the most fun arts degree you can imagine and start –

24:20.160 --> 24:21.520
You're going to get some arts degree agents.

24:22.060 --> 24:22.960
Yeah, well, that's me.

24:23.740 --> 24:31.120
But – and spend all that money going snowboarding or buying band equipment or something like that.

24:31.120 --> 24:33.360
But it's not necessarily going to pay off.

24:33.460 --> 24:38.640
You're going to, you know – so, you know, those are just personal examples of things that can go wrong.

24:39.040 --> 24:44.360
But, you know, it's about how the money is spent.

24:44.460 --> 24:48.440
And I think that's where the debate needs to be in the next few weeks.

24:48.440 --> 24:54.540
Yeah, I guess there's a lot of worry that, you know, we're borrowing for lollies to give out.

24:54.680 --> 24:58.620
We're borrowing, you know, for lots of reports and things to be done.

24:58.840 --> 25:02.540
And, you know, and the money isn't going to be used wisely.

25:02.780 --> 25:10.460
I know there's been other things like funds, growth funds that have invested in start-ups, tried to back winners when it comes, picking winners.

25:10.780 --> 25:12.700
And, you know, a lot of that hasn't panned out either.

25:12.900 --> 25:15.940
So, it's pretty difficult for governments to get it right.

25:15.940 --> 25:31.060
And, yeah, but I do think that New Zealand, given it's, you know, small size, distance away from everything, we do need – I think we do need to be prepared for the next disaster because there will be some next thing.

25:31.200 --> 25:32.960
I don't know what it's going to be.

25:33.080 --> 25:35.440
And I do think we need to get our books into a better shape.

25:35.560 --> 25:37.740
I don't know how we do that.

25:37.820 --> 25:38.900
It's not really up to me.

25:38.920 --> 25:41.220
Well, I mean, I guess it's a long-term strategy at the moment.

25:41.220 --> 25:46.080
Even nationals talking about taking several years to get debt down and under control.

25:47.300 --> 25:48.680
Labor's – I can't remember the exact numbers.

25:48.760 --> 25:55.540
Labor's talking about taking a year longer but being in a cash surplus sooner, which, again, is a sort of an accounting trick.

25:55.620 --> 26:06.500
If you think of, you know, the way you might run a household, you put it on the mortgage so that your overdraft's paid off or your overdraft's, you know, not there anymore.

26:06.500 --> 26:06.940
I don't know.

26:07.360 --> 26:24.100
We're not supposed to talk about governments as households but, I mean, it does seem like there are a whole bunch of different ways to cut it that don't really always pan out in terms of, you know, reality regardless of what the politicians are saying ahead of an election.

26:24.540 --> 26:25.200
Yeah, definitely.

26:25.200 --> 26:28.400
I mean, yeah, that's right.

26:28.540 --> 26:41.040
I mean, you know, paying down debt is good but not if you can't afford to, I don't know, do all the infrastructure that you need to build or, you know, keep people employed, that sort of thing.

26:41.040 --> 26:45.340
Yeah, they've got to think about how we keep doing that, I guess.

26:45.340 --> 26:58.780
You know, maybe, you know, some of the reasons that New Zealand, you know, precarious position on mortgage debt and agricultural debt and those things could, you know, could have put a bit more pressure on government debt.

26:58.860 --> 27:05.060
So perhaps with the private debt not looking quite so bad, that does give the government a little bit more leeway.

27:05.060 --> 27:21.380
I mean, my understanding is the ratings agencies do tend to step back and look at the whole New Zealand picture and, of course, the other thing they care about is the ability to service debt and, you know, New Zealand's still in pretty good shape on that one, as you say, you know, not too many mortgagee sales.

27:22.040 --> 27:25.760
The ag sector is booming and so those guys are in good shape.

27:25.760 --> 27:38.080
The government, you know, there are people who argue the government will face a real crunch if it doesn't deal with it, though, at some point because, you know, you're not allowed to talk about increasing taxes really either.

27:38.200 --> 27:44.240
So if you can't increase taxes, you know, how do we get the debt down and we're not going to cut services?

27:45.200 --> 27:46.660
Well, we could grow our way out.

27:46.660 --> 27:50.260
But, you know, that's what we all hope.

27:51.120 --> 27:56.340
But it's a big sort of you're putting a lot of weight on the economy, outgrowing our debt.

27:56.660 --> 28:01.140
Yeah, and obviously we have these kind of looming things like an ageing population.

28:01.600 --> 28:05.960
So health care is going to get, you know, going to cost more, superannuation is going to cost more.

28:06.080 --> 28:09.020
And those are sort of the longer term issues that we, you know, haven't solved yet.

28:09.300 --> 28:16.040
Yeah, well, you know, the longer term, the more you look longer term, the more problems there are and the more worrying it can all seem.

28:16.040 --> 28:18.940
And I think that is, you've got to be careful about that.

28:19.060 --> 28:26.620
I think that's why some of the people who, you know, send me comments and things are so worried because they are looking out to the next problem and the next problem.

28:27.640 --> 28:34.920
I think, you know, for now, I think we should, you know, we need to get the trajectory, you know, things heading in the right direction.

28:36.440 --> 28:40.260
But debt isn't quite a crisis territory.

28:40.260 --> 28:41.680
So that's good news.

28:41.680 --> 28:51.300
And, you know, hopefully we see an economic cycle turn in the next few months that just, with the added growth that just helps us get ahead.

28:52.180 --> 28:52.900
Sounds good, Liam.

28:53.420 --> 28:54.440
Got to stay positive.

28:54.800 --> 28:54.920
Yeah.

28:55.180 --> 28:55.380
Great.

28:55.520 --> 28:55.760
Thanks.

28:55.880 --> 28:56.940
Well, thanks for joining us.

28:57.080 --> 28:58.540
And thanks to CMC Markets.

28:58.660 --> 28:59.780
We'll see you here next week.

28:59.780 --> 29:00.300
Thank you.

